{"name":"Wet Cement","hex":"#89877f","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Perai."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made up of liquid or moisture, usually (but not always) water.","Of an object, etc.: covered or impregnated with liquid, usually (but not always) water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wet"},{"term":"cement","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A powdered substance produced by firing (calcining) calcium carbonate (limestone) and clay that develops strong cohesive properties when mixed with water. The main ingredient of concrete.","The paste-like substance resulting from mixing such a powder with water, or the rock-like substance that forms when it dries."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To affix with cement.","To overlay or coat with cement."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cement"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cement","blocks":[{"text":"A cement is a binder used for construction that sets, hardens, and adheres to other materials to bind them together. Cement is seldom used on its own, but rather to bind sand and gravel (aggregate) together. Cement mixed with fine aggregate produces mortar for masonry, or with sand and gravel, produces concrete. Concrete is the most widely used material in existence and is behind only water as the planet's most-consumed resource."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/USMC-110806-M-IX060-148.jpg/330px-USMC-110806-M-IX060-148.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cement"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/LDClinkerScaled.jpg/500px-LDClinkerScaled.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/LDClinkerScaled.jpg/500px-LDClinkerScaled.jpg?ut